WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.760
Adam Sandler may have made millions playing&nbsp;
lovable goofballs — but his personal life has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:10.440
been marked by sadness and tragedy. From being&nbsp;
publicly mocked by his former bosses to losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:15.400
his closest friend, the "Billy Madison" star&nbsp;
knows what it's like to be at his lowest. 
 &nbsp;

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.320
Adam Sandler landed a spot on "Saturday&nbsp;
Night Live" after proving himself as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.680
writer and appearing in a few movies and&nbsp;
television shows. His tenure on the sketch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.480
comedy series coincided with one&nbsp;
of the show's most beloved eras.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:32.440
Much to the dismay of his fans, however,&nbsp;
Sandler and Chris Farley left "SNL" after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:37.960
the 1994-95 season. At the time, audiences&nbsp;
were left thinking that the two stars had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:42.760
quit. But in recent years, Sandler has&nbsp;
exposed the truth of his exodus from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.080
late-night series. During an appearance on&nbsp;
"The Howard Stern Show," Sandler admitted,

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:52.440
"The NBC head dude, I know he didn't&nbsp;
like our gang, uh, me and Farley."

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.680
The actor also recalled how his manager let him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.000
down easy about being fired.&nbsp;
Sandler revealed to Stern:

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.200
"At the time, I was hurt because I&nbsp;
didn't know what else I was going to do."

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.880
Thankfully, Sandler became a box office&nbsp;
sensation, and holds no grudges. He even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.520
returned to host "SNL" in 2019, where he made&nbsp;
light of being fired with a musical monologue.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:15.320
Fans may look back fondly on&nbsp;
Adam Sandler's five-year run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:19.240
on "Saturday Night Live," but the actor&nbsp;
holds some deep regrets about his tenure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:24.480
on the show. In an interview with AARP,&nbsp;
Sandler reminisced about his "SNL" days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.440
and expressed disappointment with much&nbsp;
of his early conduct. He told the outlet,

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:32.560
"I used to go nuts. I had a quick&nbsp;
temper, quick reactions. I made a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.680
of dumb mistakes and said a lot of stupid&nbsp;
things. Looking back on relationships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:41.120
I could be an ass. I was selfish. I&nbsp;
was competitive with other comedians."

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.320
The actor also admitted that his&nbsp;
aggressive pursuit of fame left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.520
him unable to acknowledge the&nbsp;
talent of his peers. He added,

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.280
"I was hungry, I didn't always see&nbsp;
clearly then. I wanted to be a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.800
comedian like Eddie Murphy,&nbsp;
like Rodney Dangerfield."

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.200
Thankfully, Sandler has matured&nbsp;
a lot since his "SNL" days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.440
and is no longer as competitive&nbsp;
as he once was. He told AARP,

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:07.320
"I appreciate other people's talent now rather&nbsp;
than competing. I'll watch somebody and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.640
'Man, they're great. I never would have&nbsp;
thought of that joke or that approach.'"

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.400
Even though it isn't his first movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:19.120
1995's "Billy Madison" is often credited as&nbsp;
launching Adam Sandler's huge film career.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.160
"Stop looking at me, Swan!"

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:26.240
The movie is about a man-child forced to complete&nbsp;
several grade school classes in order to secure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.320
his father's estate. It was also the first film&nbsp;
Sandler co-wrote with his screenwriting partner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:36.480
Tim Herlihy. "Billy Madison" is considered a&nbsp;
classic today, but at the time of its release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.320
critics were not on board with Sandler's&nbsp;
immature antics and obnoxious energy. Notably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:45.200
Roger Ebert used the opportunity to&nbsp;
focus on the actor's screen presence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.840
comparing him to fingernails on a blackboard.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.360
Sadly, Sandler was ill-prepared for the barrage&nbsp;
of negative comments, telling Deadline in 2022,

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:56.800
"When 'Billy Madison' came out, me and my&nbsp;
friend who wrote it [...] read the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.800
[review] and we were like, 'Oh my god, what&nbsp;
happened? They hate us.' And then we were like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.680
'It must have been this paper,' but then 90%&nbsp;
of the papers are going, 'This is garbage.'"

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:08.520
"I did stop reading them after that."

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:13.520
Since then, Sandler has been pretty hostile&nbsp;
towards critics and the media in general. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.360
Adam Sandler has developed a notorious reputation&nbsp;
for avoiding interviews with the media — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:23.040
for good reason. The comedian can pinpoint a&nbsp;
couple of events early in his career when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.880
was misquoted, which resulted in his distrust for&nbsp;
journalists. He told The Harvard Crimson in 2000:

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.280
"One time I was in a room with a&nbsp;
person who kept going on and on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.400
about how much he loved the movie.&nbsp;
That night, I see him on TV saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.960
'This movie is useless.' And I'm like,&nbsp;
'Dude! That's the guy who was nice to me.'"

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.760
Another example of how the media has done&nbsp;
Sandler wrong came up during a 2023 interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.600
with the Washington Post. In the interview,&nbsp;
Sandler recalled a moment in 1995 when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:56.040
was quoted in TV Guide for saying the writing&nbsp;
on SNL was bad. Sandler recalled to the Post:

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:01.160
"I go, 'I never f---ing said that. I never&nbsp;
would say that.' I called the writer. I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.880
'Why did you say I said that?'&nbsp;
And he kind of didn't want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.480
talk to me. I should have taped the conversation."

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:11.120
Since then, the actor has spent most of his&nbsp;
career avoiding interviews and critics. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.320
he admitted to AARP that he's still&nbsp;
hurt by negative reviews, saying,

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:19.840
"Mostly because I invite all these amazing&nbsp;
people I care about to make movies with me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:23.280
and I wish they didn't have to read&nbsp;
s--- about whatever we've made."

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.680
At the height of his film career in 1999, Sandler&nbsp;
started Happy Madison Productions. From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.780
he struck a long-term deal with&nbsp;
Sony Pictures, which saw him star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:37.240
in 24 films for the company. However, things&nbsp;
didn't end so well between Sandler and Sony.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:42.480
In 2014, Sandler signed a multi-picture deal&nbsp;
with Netflix, which essentially put an end to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.920
his relationship with Sony. With a boatload of&nbsp;
money coming his way from the streaming service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:51.360
the maneuver seemed like a natural transition.&nbsp;
However, news soon came to light that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:56.080
was far more going on behind the scenes&nbsp;
between Sandler and his former distributor.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:02.120
After malicious hackers raided Sony Pictures in&nbsp;
2014, emails featuring Sandler's name surfaced.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:07.520
In discussions between Sony's heads, the comedic&nbsp;
powerhouse was described as a self-important jerk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.560
whose best days were behind him. The actor&nbsp;
has never publicly addressed his falling out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:17.640
with Sony or the emails, but after a longstanding&nbsp;
and fruitful relationship, that one had to hurt.

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:22.760
Adam Sandler would be the first person to admit&nbsp;
that he is not perfect. And throughout his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:26.160
the actor has described many of the&nbsp;
personal challenges that he has had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.880
to overcome. During an interview&nbsp;
with The Harvard Crimson in 2000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.040
the comedian admitted that he&nbsp;
has an anger problem, saying,

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.640
"In real life, I do have a bit of&nbsp;
that problem. But over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:38.560
I've been getting better I think."

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.760
Sandler also said he relates to&nbsp;
the "loser" characters he often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:45.240
depicts on screen. He admitted&nbsp;
on the "Little Gold Men" podcast,

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:49.240
"I just have a natural part of my brain&nbsp;
that feels like I don't belong here. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:53.120
feeling uncomfortable and loser stuff&nbsp;
I've been doing for years, it's in me."

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:58.120
In recent years, Sandler has been proactive&nbsp;
in treating his mental health, telling AARP,

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:03.120
"I talk to a shrink sometimes. He's given me&nbsp;
a plan. Sometimes just holding in a sentence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:08.120
taking a beat for a minute before saying something&nbsp;
stupid. When I do that, I realize it wasn't that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:10.534
important to say it in the first place."

00:06:10.534 --> 00:06:13.301
With fame comes consequence. For Adam Sandler, 

00:06:13.301 --> 00:06:17.040
that means he spends a lot of time&nbsp;
dealing with legal disputes. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:22.160
Sandler's 2008 film "You Don't Mess With&nbsp;
the Zohan" landed the actor in hot water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:26.800
after cartoonist Robert Cabell claimed that&nbsp;
the lead character was plagiarized. The film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:31.000
which is about an Israeli counter-terrorist&nbsp;
commando turned hairdresser, did share many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:34.800
similarities with Cabell's cartoon&nbsp;
"Jayms Blonde" In Cabell's cartoon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:38.480
the lead character is a Navy SEALs-trained&nbsp;
hair stylist who fights crime.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:43.960
In 2013, Sandler and company were sued again by a&nbsp;
carpenter who lost his finger while building sets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:48.360
for "That's My Boy." The complaint sought&nbsp;
$1 million for "gross negligence" after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:53.440
Sandler's production company failed to provide&nbsp;
the proper safety equipment required for the job.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:58.000
But Sandler's most drawn-out case involved&nbsp;
a professional nanny named Deanne McDonald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.120
who he hired to care for his children.&nbsp;
McDonald originally settled a dispute with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:06.680
Sandler out of court. The suit involved&nbsp;
undisclosed employment claims. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:12.520
after breaching a clause in the settlement,&nbsp;
McDonald was ordered to repay the actor $80,000. 

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:16.720
Since he's no longer a young performer,&nbsp;
Sandler has felt the signs of aging. The actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:20.880
started getting concerned about his physical&nbsp;
health while filming back-to-back features,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:25.720
"Murder Mystery 2" and his upcoming&nbsp;
movie, "Spaceman." He told Variety:

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:30.320
"I was hanging in a harness all the time. I&nbsp;
kept saying, 'Something's going on with my hip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:35.040
man.' I was like, 'Yeah, I definitely gotta&nbsp;
x-ray that thing,' and we were in trouble."

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:38.520
The actor underwent hip surgery in September 2022&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:42.040
and was understandably in a lot&nbsp;
of agony for some time after.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:44.200
"My hip surgery was painful."

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:47.920
Sandler also admitted that the&nbsp;
surgery made him feel "old." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:51.600
when asked about how he felt after&nbsp;
surgery, the actor joked to "Page Six":

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:54.680
"I screamed a lot then calmed&nbsp;
down and ate some food."
 &nbsp;

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:59.120
It has become somewhat of a tradition for Adam&nbsp;
Sandler to cast his friends in his productions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:03.760
After all, "Grown Ups" and its sequel were&nbsp;
essentially conceptualized as an opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:07.920
to spend more time with his buddies. So,&nbsp;
there is no doubt that camaraderie is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:12.720
core value to Sandler. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
with over 70 film credits to his name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.680
the actor has had to say goodbye to many&nbsp;
co-stars throughout the years. Some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:21.880
notable friends of Sandler who have passed&nbsp;
away include Bob Barker and Cloris Leachman.

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:25.680
There are a couple of deaths that hit&nbsp;
Sandler harder than others. Norm MacDonald,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:30.760
who shared the stage with Sandler on "SNL" and had&nbsp;
his first movie credit on "Billy Madison," passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:36.640
away in 2021. After Norm's passing, a heartbroken&nbsp;
Sandler shared on X, formerly known as Twitter:

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:40.960
"Every one of us loved Norm. Some of the&nbsp;
hardest laughs of my life with this man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:46.560
Most fearless funny original guy we knew. An&nbsp;
incredible dad. A great friend. A legend."

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:50.680
Still, the most heartbreaking loss for Sandler&nbsp;
was the untimely passing of Chris Farley in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:57.000
1997. After spending time on "SNL" together,&nbsp;
Farley was one of Sandler's closest confidants.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.901
"Ravioli? Holy cannoli."

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:03.400
Sandler still misses his former&nbsp;
co-star and memorialized him with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:08.160
a four-minute tribute song during his&nbsp;
hosting appearance on "SNL" in 2019.

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.400
"Something, just something very&nbsp;
powerful that night. And it felt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:14.585
felt great talking about Chris."

00:09:14.585 --> 00:09:16.120
There is one thing that Adam Sandler 

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:20.840
values above all else: Family. The actor gives&nbsp;
much of the credit for his success to the endless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:26.200
support he received growing up. While accepting&nbsp;
the 2023 Mark Twain Prize for American Humor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:29.400
the comedian said there was one&nbsp;
thing that led him to the night:

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:31.360
"I come from a great family."

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:35.880
Tragically, Adam Sandler was hit with a&nbsp;
significant loss in 2003, when his father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:40.440
Stanley, passed away. The actor has held much&nbsp;
respect for his father throughout his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:46.160
even using him as inspiration for his character&nbsp;
in 2022's "Hustle." He said to ET Canada:

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:50.000
"I was just basically doing my dad when&nbsp;
he used to coach my teams growing up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:53.560
his kind of demeanor. He'd push&nbsp;
you but then he'd also say like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:57.101
'All right, I see my kid's about to&nbsp;
snap, let me pull back a little bit."

00:09:57.101 --> 00:10:01.600
Understandably, the former "SNL" star&nbsp;
was devastated by the loss of his father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:05.120
especially after watching his&nbsp;
health decline. Sandler told Parade:

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:07.520
"I went through stuff with my dad before he died.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:11.000
I saw firsthand what goes on with&nbsp;
people who are incredibly sick."

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:12.960
The actor still holds a special place in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:16.720
heart for Stanley Sandler. In&nbsp;
a 2020 Facebook post, he wrote,

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:21.680
"My dad. The greatest man I ever knew.&nbsp;
Love you so dad! I know I was a mistake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:25.429
but I know you had a good time! Thanks&nbsp;
for having me and miss you every day!"

